//? Title: MAF: Parallel Density and Sequential Inversion
//? Subtitle: Strict degree masks, actual conditioner Jacobians and a preserved banana fit
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A Gaussian autoregression predicts each coordinate's mean and log scale from
strictly earlier coordinates. Its inverse maps an observation to independent
Gaussian coordinates, with a triangular Jacobian and tractable likelihood.
Stacking such maps gives a masked autoregressive flow. This native C++ study
checks a hand-set strict conditioner and dependency-ordered inverse before
fitting the original four-stage, 466-value teaching model to procedural banana
pools. The complete program retains every degree mask, all 48 Adam writes,
both actual map Jacobians, conditioner Jacobians and all 1,024 inverse samples.
The fixed-final test NLL is 2.878228426 nats per two-coordinate row, better than
the fitting-only full Gaussian's 2.994631823, while analytic truth scores
2.796480843. Source reproduction establishes this experiment's fidelity;
it does not establish performance of a large, tuned density estimator.
)
\toc

> Use strictly earlier coordinates to make likelihood parallel

[Papamakarios, Pavlakou and Murray, Masked Autoregressive Flow for Density
Estimation (2017)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07057) stacks autoregressive
transformations for density estimation. The conditioner uses the degree masks
of [Germain et al., MADE (2015)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.03509) to exclude
current and later inputs. For one ordered block of our compact model,

$$ z_c=(x_c-\mu_c(x_{<c}))\exp(-s_c(x_{<c})),\qquad
\log|\det Df(x)|=-\sum_c s_c(x_{<c}). $$
\alttext(Each observed coordinate is centered and scaled using functions of strictly earlier observed coordinates. The triangular Jacobian's diagonal is exp of the negative log scale, so its log determinant is minus the sum of log scales.)

All coordinates of x are supplied, so one masked conditioner call produces
all means and log scales in parallel. Strict exclusion makes the Jacobian
triangular in dependency order: the conditioner cannot add a derivative on
the diagonal, leaving exp(−s_c)>0. Reversing the order permutes both matrix
axes; its determinant remains positive. The source alternates orders (0,1)
and (1,0), with a 2->16 tanh->4 conditioner in every block. Heads are
mu0,mu1,raw_s0,raw_s1, and **s=.8tanh(raw_s)** bounds each log scale.
These dimensions, fixed orders and bound describe the migrated teaching model.

Inversion has a different dependency. Given z, recover the first x coordinate
from its constant heads, recompute the conditioner on that recovered prefix,
then recover the second:

$$ x_c=z_c\exp(s_c(x_{<c}))+\mu_c(x_{<c}). $$
\alttext(Inverse generation recovers coordinates sequentially because each later mean and log scale requires earlier recovered observation coordinates.)

The native inverse therefore uses two conditioner calls per two-coordinate
block, while density uses one. It computes its own determinant along recovered
points; it does not cache heads from an unrelated forward observation. Four
blocks contribute their per-row negative sums, then a learned global positive
diagonal scale contributes sum(log_scale). With final Gaussian coordinate h,

$$ \log p_X(x)=-\log(2\pi)-\tfrac12\|h\|^2
-\sum_{i=0}^3\sum_{c=0}^1 s_{i,c}+\sum_{c=0}^1\mathrm{log\_scale}_c. $$
\alttext(The final observation log density combines Gaussian log density, four autoregressive negative log-scale sums and the final positive diagonal scale's log determinant.)

The objective is negative mean log density, in nats per two-coordinate row.
Sampling supplies Gaussian h, unscales it and reverses blocks3,2,1,0, recovering
each block's coordinates in its own order. The report keeps fitting and sampling
visible; the core API owns reusable numerical maps and their live graphs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/maf_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::Diagram d(28,1150);
 dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle s;s.width=10;s.height=5;s.font_size=32;
 d.node("split",{-24,0},"Strict degree masks\nNo own/future input",s)
 .node("shift",{-11,0},"Heads mu,s\n2 -> 16 tanh -> 4",s)
 .node("add",{2,0},"Parallel density\n(x - mu) exp(-s)",s)
 .node("stack",{15,0},"4 ordered blocks\nPositive global scale",s)
 .node("density",{28,0},"Gaussian base\nAdd -sum(s) + scale",s)
 .edge("split","shift").edge("shift","add").edge("add","stack").edge("stack","density")
 .save_svg("build/maf_method.svg");
}
